                <h2>Case Recap: Account Restricted Immediately After Recharge</h2>
<p>At 3 AM in September 2024, the Yiyu customer service team received an urgent message: a utility app's Japan market advertising account showed "payment function limited," while the account balance still had approximately $28,000. The client had just completed the recharge the day before and hadn't started formal advertising yet. This incident later became known internally as the "09 Event" and became an important node for iterating the internal compliance audit SOP.</p>
<h2>Problem Analysis: Hidden Risks Buried Before Recharge</h2>
<p>After review, we found that the "09 Event" wasn't triggered by the recharge operation itself—the account had accumulated multiple compliance risks before recharging:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Creative Compliance Issue</strong>: The client's advertising creatives for the Japanese market contained the expression "Get Premium membership free upon installation," which falls under "inducement installation" violations explicitly prohibited by Meta's review policy.</li>
<li><strong>Missing Domain Verification</strong>: The domain used for the ad landing page hadn't completed Meta's domain verification and was marked as "unverified domain" in the system.</li>
<li><strong>Targeting Region Mismatch</strong>: Some IP segments classified as high-risk review regions by Meta were included in the audience targeting.</li>
</ul>
<p>These three issues wouldn't immediately trigger penalties when existing alone. During the "dormant period" when the account balance was zero, the system wouldn't proactively scan for risks. Once recharged, the account status switched from "dormant" to "active," and the system re-evaluated the overall account risk score, leading to payment function restrictions.</p>
<h2>Judgment Logic: How to Prioritize Compliance Risks</h2>
<p>Not all compliance issues require emergency handling. The Yiyu team established a three-tier judgment mechanism:</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Dimension</th>
<th>High Priority</th>
<th>Medium-Low Priority</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Asset Risk</td>
<td>High balance, wide coverage, large audience</td>
<td>Small accounts, single-region targeting</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Violation Type</td>
<td>Payment-related, privacy data, political sensitive content</td>
<td>Clickbait, creative specifications</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Trigger Timing</td>
<td>Post-recharge, pre-withdrawal, review period</td>
<td>Normal stable advertising period</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>The "09 Event" was judged as high priority because the account balance exceeded $10,000 and involved inducement-type violations—a key focus of Meta's review.</p>
<h2>Handling Actions: Emergency Response Within 72 Hours</h2>
<p>The team immediately initiated the emergency process after discovering the problem:</p>
<h3>Step 1: Account Isolation</h3>
<p>Suspend new recharge operations for this account, isolate the remaining balance from new funds to avoid expanding losses. Contact the client to explain the situation and reassure them.</p>
<h3>Step 2: Creative and Landing Page Rectification</h3>
<ul>
<li>Remove all creative expressions involving "free membership"</li>
<li>Assist the client in completing domain verification, update the privacy policy page URL in the ad manager</li>
<li>Temporarily close audience targeting for high-risk regions</li>
</ul>
<h3>Step 3: Appeal and Follow-up</h3>
<p>Submit an appeal according to Meta Business Support's standard template, attaching rectification records and a compliance commitment letter. The team arranged a dedicated person to refresh the account status every hour.</p>
<p><strong>Result</strong>: Account payment function restored within 48 hours, remaining balance unaffected, appeal approved. The client stated at the review meeting: "If I had known about the pre-recharge compliance check, this incident could have been completely avoided."</p>
<h2>Standardized SOP: From Case to Process</h2>
<p>After the "09 Event," we solidified the emergency handling experience into a standard audit SOP applicable to all Yiyu-managed accounts:</p>
<h3>Pre-Recharge Checklist</h3>
<ol>
<li><strong>Domain Verification Status</strong>: Confirm domain ownership verification completed in the Business Manager</li>
<li><strong>Qualification Certification</strong>: Check if business license and advertiser qualifications are within validity period</li>
<li><strong>Target Region Compliance</strong>: Verify if target markets have special review requirements</li>
<li><strong>Creative Pre-check</strong>: Conduct internal review of proposed ad copy, images, and videos</li>
<li><strong>Privacy Policy</strong>: Confirm landing page includes privacy statements compliant with GDPR, CCPA, and other regulations</li>
<li><strong>Recharge Threshold Alert</strong>: Set account balance warning value to automatically trigger audit reminders when exceeded</li>
</ol>
<h3>The "Three No-Recharge" Principle</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>No recharge if domain unverified</strong></li>
<li><strong>No recharge if qualifications expired</strong></li>
<li><strong>No recharge if high-risk regions unplanned</strong></li>
</ul>
<h3>Daily Audit Mechanism</h3>
<ul>
<li>Conduct compliance status spot checks on active accounts weekly</li>
<li>Complete re-review within 24 hours after creative updates</li>
<li>High-risk region targeting requires additional risk assessment form submission</li>
</ul>
<h2>Lessons Learned</h2>
<p>Compliance issues never "appear when problems occur"—they "exist all along, waiting to be triggered." Accounts appear safe at zero balance, but once recharged and activated, all historical hidden risks will be surfaced by the system. A complete compliance audit in advance might only take 2 hours; but emergency appeals after account restrictions often require over 48 hours of effort.</p>
<p>The key to establishing a compliance audit SOP isn't "eliminating all risks in the bud" but "keeping risks always in a discoverable and controllable state." Rather than waiting for account bans to handle emergencies, overseas marketing teams should make audits a working habit.</p>
<p>If you're managing multiple overseas advertising accounts or just preparing to recharge and launch for a new market, welcome to exchange compliance audit practical experience with the Yiyu team.</p>
